---
import { sitePath } from '../lib/paths';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LAB_NAME } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="About Our Lab" description={`About the ${LAB_NAME} at Tufts University.`}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap lab-page">
      <h1>About Our Lab</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The {LAB_NAME}, in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Tufts
        University, studies how climate extremes affect infrastructure and the communities that
        depend on it, and how both can adapt.
      </p>

      <h2>Challenge</h2>
      <p>
        Droughts, floods, wildfires, and sea level rise are becoming more frequent and severe,
        increasingly overlapping or triggering one another. These compound and cascading hazards
        strain protective structures such as levees, dams, slopes, and coastal bluffs, while
        threatening the lifeline networks and essential services that communities depend on daily.
        The impacts are unevenly distributed: disadvantaged communities often face higher exposure
        with fewer resources and lower adaptive capacity to prepare, respond, and recover.
      </p>

      <h2>Research Focus</h2>
      <p>
        Our research connects four areas. At the structural level, we model earthen infrastructure
        such as levees and dams, including developing hybrid digital twins for real-time
        monitoring and risk assessment. At the hazard level, we characterize climate extremes and
        their impacts, from wildfire behavior to rapid transitions between drought and heavy rain.
        In infrastructure equity, we examine disparities in shelter access, risk communication,
        and contaminated-site cleanup to ensure adaptation benefits underserved populations.
        Finally, we partner directly with communities on local resilience. Our collaborations
        include monitoring coastal bluff stability with Indigenous partners, safeguarding food
        supply networks against cascading climate disruptions, and advancing resilient, affordable
        housing in climate-vulnerable rural areas.
      </p>

      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        We integrate physics-based modeling of soils and earthen structures with remote sensing,
        statistics, and machine learning. We pair these models with spatial and socioeconomic
        analyses of vulnerability and service access. At the intersection of geotechnical
        engineering, climate science, hydrology, data science, and social science, we work closely
        with public agencies and local communities to turn research into practical adaptation.
      </p>

      <p class="about-actions">
        The lab is led by <a href={sitePath('/about')}>Prof. Farshid Vahedifard</a>. Our
        <a href={sitePath('/projects')}>projects</a> and
        <a href={sitePath('/publications')}>publications</a> describe this work in more detail, and
        we welcome <a href={sitePath('/join')}>inquiries</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
